<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maputo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lourenço Marques</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maputo, known until 1976 as Lourenço Marques, is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a> in the far south of the country. A Portuguese trading post grew into a town that replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique-island">Mozambique Island</a> as the colonial capital in 1898, helped by the railway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> opened in 1895. It became the capital of independent Mozambique in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maputo.jpg" alt="Maputo" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AmigoDia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maputo_seen_from_southeast_-_October_2006_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…apartheid included the communist lawyer Bram Fischer, who defended Mandela at Rivonia and died a prisoner, the writer Ruth First, killed by a parcel bomb sent by the security police to <b>Maputo</b> in 1982, and Helen Suzman, for thirteen years the only member of her party in parliament.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The settlers built Fort Lijdzaamheid on the site of modern <b>Maputo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-delagoa-bay">Dutch Delagoa Bay</a></li><li><blockquote>Effective occupation came only after the defeat of the Gaza ruler Gungunhana in 1895, and the capital moved to <b>Lourenco Marques</b> in 1898.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a></li></ul></section>
